WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.096
This lesson becomes much easier when momentum is treated as a system quantity with signs.

2
00:00:06.096 --> 00:00:12.191
Carry Score depends on both mass and velocity, so a heavy slow craft and a light fast craft can carry comparable momentum.

3
00:00:12.191 --> 00:00:18.287
During a closed docking event, the total system momentum is what stays fixed.

4
00:00:18.287 --> 00:00:24.382
That means the reasoning must begin with the total before the collision, not with guesses about how fast one object should keep moving afterward.

5
00:00:24.382 --> 00:00:30.478
Keep this takeaway in view: Momentum conservation belongs to the whole closed system, not to one object by itself.

6
00:00:30.478 --> 00:00:36.573
Carry Score means momentum.

7
00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:42.669
Momentum combines mass and velocity in one signed quantity.

8
00:00:42.669 --> 00:00:48.764
System view means total conserved.

9
00:00:48.764 --> 00:00:54.860
In a closed system, the total momentum before equals the total momentum after.

10
00:00:54.860 --> 00:01:01.600
The docking picture helps because it shows two incoming motion stories merging into one shared final motion.

11
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:08.340
Before the collision, each craft carries its own signed momentum.

12
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:15.080
After they stick together, the combined mass must share the same total system momentum if the event is closed.

13
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:21.820
The lesson is not saying each craft keeps its old momentum.

14
00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:28.560
It is saying the total for the whole system is conserved across the event.

15
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:35.300
Keep this takeaway in view: The total momentum survives the docking event, even though the motion is redistributed afterward.

16
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:42.040
Before docking: Each craft carries its own signed momentum contribution.

17
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:48.780
Closed system: No significant external force means the total momentum stays fixed.

18
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:54.554
A helpful analogy is a docking bay with signed motion tokens.

19
00:01:54.554 --> 00:02:00.328
One craft can bring positive tokens, another can bring negative tokens if it moves the opposite way.

20
00:02:00.328 --> 00:02:06.102
When they dock, the bay keeps the total token balance.

21
00:02:06.102 --> 00:02:11.876
What changes is how that total is now shared across the combined mass.

22
00:02:11.876 --> 00:02:17.650
This analogy helps because it keeps conservation tied to the full closed bay, not to the idea that each craft must preserve its original motion.

23
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:23.424
Keep this takeaway in view: Use signed system totals, not isolated object instincts, when reading docking problems.

24
00:02:23.424 --> 00:02:29.198
Positive tokens means positive momentum.

25
00:02:29.198 --> 00:02:34.972
Motion in the chosen positive direction counts positively.

26
00:02:34.972 --> 00:02:40.746
Negative tokens means negative momentum.

27
00:02:40.746 --> 00:02:46.520
Motion in the opposite direction subtracts from the total.

28
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:52.855
The core relations are momentum equals mass times velocity and total momentum before equals total momentum after.

29
00:02:52.855 --> 00:02:59.189
The first relation tells you how to calculate each object's contribution.

30
00:02:59.189 --> 00:03:05.524
The second tells you what stays fixed for the closed system.

31
00:03:05.524 --> 00:03:11.858
If objects stick together, the final step is often to divide the conserved total momentum by the combined mass to find the shared final velocity.

32
00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:18.193
But that division only makes sense after the total system momentum has already been established correctly.

33
00:03:18.193 --> 00:03:24.527
Keep this takeaway in view: Find each signed momentum first, then conserve the system total.

34
00:03:24.527 --> 00:03:30.862
Mass matters: More mass at the same velocity gives more momentum.

35
00:03:30.862 --> 00:03:37.196
Velocity matters: A larger velocity at the same mass gives more momentum.

36
00:03:37.196 --> 00:03:43.531
p = mv.

37
00:03:43.531 --> 00:03:49.865
total p before = total p after.

38
00:03:49.865 --> 00:03:56.200
Choose a sign convention first, then calculate each momentum contribution before adding them.

39
00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:01.704
Because momentum includes velocity, it inherits the sign of the chosen direction.

40
00:04:01.704 --> 00:04:07.209
If east is positive, an eastward momentum is positive and a westward momentum is negative.

41
00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:12.713
So when one craft moves east and the other moves west, the total is found by signed addition, not by adding magnitudes blindly.

42
00:04:12.713 --> 00:04:18.218
This step often determines whether the final direction is predicted correctly.

43
00:04:18.218 --> 00:04:23.722
Keep this takeaway in view: Signed momentum addition is the gatekeeper step for correct docking answers.

44
00:04:23.722 --> 00:04:29.227
Choose positive means before calculating.

45
00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:34.731
The sign convention must be chosen at the start.

46
00:04:34.731 --> 00:04:40.236
Same direction means add positively.

47
00:04:40.236 --> 00:04:45.740
Momenta pointing the same way reinforce one another.

48
00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:51.130
A common trap is to assume one craft should somehow keep its speed after docking because momentum is conserved.

49
00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:56.520
That is not what the law says.

50
00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.910
It is the total system momentum that stays fixed, not the individual speed or momentum of each craft.

51
00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:07.300
Another trap is to swap in force language because the collision feels violent.

52
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:12.690
The calculation here is a momentum conservation story, not a force-size story.

53
00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:18.080
Keep this takeaway in view: Conservation is a whole-system rule, not an object-by-object rule.

54
00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.470
Conserve the total system momentum, not one craft's speed.

55
00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:28.860
Use signs on the momenta before adding them.

56
00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:32.455
Check yourself.

57
00:05:32.455 --> 00:05:36.051
What two quantities make up momentum?

58
00:05:36.051 --> 00:05:39.646
Mass and velocity.

59
00:05:39.646 --> 00:05:43.242
What is conserved in a closed docking event?

60
00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:46.837
The total system momentum.

61
00:05:46.837 --> 00:05:50.432
If one craft has positive momentum and another has negative momentum, what do you do?

62
00:05:50.432 --> 00:05:54.028
Add them with signs to get the system total.

63
00:05:54.028 --> 00:05:57.623
Those three answers are enough to unlock the worked example.

64
00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:01.218
Keep this takeaway in view: Think system total first, then compute the shared final motion.

65
00:06:01.218 --> 00:06:04.814
Momentum means mass x velocity.

66
00:06:04.814 --> 00:06:08.409
Momentum always depends on both load and motion.

67
00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:12.005
Conserved item means system total.

68
00:06:12.005 --> 00:06:15.600
The full closed system keeps the same total momentum.

69
00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:22.005
You are ready for the worked example now.

70
00:06:22.005 --> 00:06:28.410
Take east as positive.

71
00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:34.815
The three kilogram craft moving east at four metres per second has plus twelve kilogram metres per second of momentum.

72
00:06:34.815 --> 00:06:41.220
The one kilogram craft moving west at two metres per second has minus two kilogram metres per second.

73
00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:47.625
Add them to get plus ten kilogram metres per second before docking.

74
00:06:47.625 --> 00:06:54.030
After they stick together, the combined mass is four kilograms, so the shared final velocity is ten divided by four, which is two point five metres per second east.

75
00:06:54.030 --> 00:07:00.435
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: signed momentum totals lead directly to the final shared motion.

76
00:07:00.435 --> 00:07:06.840
Step 1: Calculate each signed momentum separately.

77
00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:13.245
Step 2: Add them to get the conserved system total before docking.

78
00:07:13.245 --> 00:07:19.650
p_total = +12 + (-2) = +10 kg m/s.

79
00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:26.055
combined mass = 4 kg.

80
00:07:26.055 --> 00:07:32.460
Signed system total first, combined-mass sharing second.
